# Ticket 009 - Incorrect Default Gateway

## Issue

PC1 could communicate with devices on its local subnet but could not reliably communicate with devices in other subnets.

## Symptoms

PC1 could ping PC2 in the same subnet.

PC1 could reach its correct router interface.

PC1 could not reach PC3 in VLAN 20. (Because of packet tracer, it was sitll able to reach PC3 but in a regualr network it would not be able to)

## Investigation

Tested connectivity using ping.

Confirmed PC1's IP address and subnet mask.

Checked PC1's configured default gateway in Desktop → IP Configuration.

Found that the gateway was set to 192.168.0.65 instead of 192.168.0.1.

## Root Cause

PC1 was configured with the default gateway for VLAN 20 rather than the router interface for VLAN 10.

## Resolution

Changed PC1's default gateway to 192.168.0.1, leaving its IP address and subnet mask unchanged.

## Verification

Retested connectivity to PC2, PC1's correct default gateway, and PC3. Connectivity was restored.

## Lesson Learned

A PC uses its default gateway to reach destinations outside its local subnet. An incorrect gateway can prevent off-subnet communication even when the PC's IP address, subnet mask, cable, and local VLAN are correct.
